<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Morava</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Morava is the principal river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, formed by the meeting of the South and West Morava and flowing north about 185 kilometres to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Its valley is the main route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> between the Danube and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. In the late 14th century it was the heart of Prince Lazar's Moravian Serbia, and it gave its name to the Morava school of medieval church architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-morava.jpg" alt="Great Morava" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Borjan Zlatkovic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morava_river2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
